rsatile range, playing everything from tough cops to affable priests. Some of his most notable roles include Father Duffy in \"The Fighting 69th\" (1940), George Simon in \"The Sullivans\" (1944), and Knobby Walsh in \"Angels with Dirty Faces\" (1938). He was also a regular fixture in many classic Warner Bros. Films of the 1930s and 1940s, working opposite stars like James Cagney and Humphrey Bogart.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nCan you tell me more about Pat O'Brien's signature style or any notable autographs he left behind?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nPat O'Brien is known to have had a very distinctive signature style. His autograph was very stylized and often featured swooping, looping letters that were slanted at an angle. He signed his full name \"Pat O'Brien\" in flowing letters that often connected to form a strong visual diagonal line across the page. His autographs were highly sought after by fans and collectors alike, and are considered to be among the most iconic and recognizable in Hollywood history. Many of his autographs survive to this day, with signed photos, letters, and other memorabilia selling for high prices at auction. One particularly notable autograph is a signed photo of O'Brien and Humphrey Bogart from the set of \"The Barefoot Contessa\" (1954), which sold at auction for over $2,000.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nCan you provide me with any information on Pat O'Brien's personal life or background?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nSure! Pat O'Brien was born William Joseph Patrick O'Brien on November 11, 1899, in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. He was the son of Irish Catholic immigrants and grew up in a large, working-class family. O'Brien attended Marquette University and began his career as a journalist before transitioning to acting. He got his start in theater before moving to Hol","tokens":["<|im_start|>","system","\n","You"," are"," a"," helpful"," assistant",".","<|im_end|>","\n","<|im_start|>","user","\n","Given"," the"," text",":"," PA